 "pd": {
  "method_card": {
   "steps": [
    "Pie chart angle = (value / total) x 360",
    "Reading a pie chart: value = (angle / 360) x total",
    "Histogram: check if class widths are equal",
    "Equal widths: frequency = bar height",
    "Unequal widths: frequency = density x class width"
   ],
   "title": "Pie Charts & Histograms",
   "content": "<p><strong>Pie charts</strong> show proportions of a whole. The full circle represents 100% (360°). Each sector's angle is proportional to the percentage it represents. To calculate an angle: divide the value by the total, then multiply by 360. Pie charts are widely used in geography to show employment structure, energy mix, land use and ethnic composition.</p><p><strong>Histograms</strong> look similar to bar charts but show <strong>continuous data</strong> grouped into class intervals. Unlike bar charts, bars touch (no gaps) because the data is continuous. In histograms with <strong>equal class widths</strong>, the height shows the frequency. In histograms with <strong>unequal class widths</strong>, the y-axis shows frequency density (frequency ÷ class width).</p><p><strong>Reading a pie chart:</strong> To find the actual value a sector represents, calculate: (sector angle ÷ 360) × total, or (sector percentage ÷ 100) × total.</p><p><strong>Constructing a pie chart:</strong> Calculate each angle using (value ÷ total) × 360. Draw each sector using a protractor. Always label sectors or include a key.</p>",
   "example": "<p><strong>Question:</strong> In a survey, 120 people were asked about their main mode of transport. Car = 50%, Bus = 25%, Walk = 15%, Cycle = 10%. Calculate the angle for the 'Car' sector.</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Angle = (percentage ÷ 100) × 360 = (50 ÷ 100) × 360</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> = 0.5 × 360 = 180°</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 180°</p>"
  },
